<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Serbs</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Serbs are a South Slavic people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christian. Large Serb communities lived within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>. Accounts of the Austro-Hungarian Empire list them among the nationalities of the multinational empire, and Serb nationalism was a major source of tension before 1914.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The origin of the name Serb is unclear. The most prominent theory holds that it is of Proto-Slavic origin, meaning family kinship or alliance, and ultimately rooted in a word for sipping or breast-feeding, so that it carries the idea of milk kinship and brotherhood. Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, especially the Sclaveni and Antae, including the White Serbs, invaded and settled Southeastern Europe in the 6th and 7th centuries. Until the late 560s they mainly raided across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, but large-scale settlement overwhelmed the Danube and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> frontier in the late 6th and early 7th centuries. The region of Raška was the centre of Serb settlement, and Serb tribes also occupied most of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> and Montenegro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first Serb states, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> (c. 780–960) and Duklja (10th century to 1186/9), were formed chiefly under the Vlastimirović and Vojislavljević dynasties. As Duklja declined in the late 11th century, Raška gained independence and became the most powerful Serbian state. Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a>, (r. 1166–96), founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemanjic-dynasty">Nemanjić dynasty</a>, which ruled Serbia until the 14th century. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-the-first-crowned">Stefan Nemanjić</a> became the first recognised king, and his younger son Rastko founded the Serbian Orthodox Church in 1219 and became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sava">Saint Sava</a>.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">devshirme</a> took Christian boys from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, many of them Serbs, from their families, converted them by force to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and trained them for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissary</a> corps. Some Serbs who converted rose to the highest ranks, among them the grand vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokollu-mehmed-pasha">Sokollu Mehmed Pasha</a>. The Serbian Revolution (1804–1817) began with the First Serbian Uprising under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karadorde">Karađorđe Petrović</a>, which created an independent state for almost a decade until the Ottomans crushed it in 1813. The Second Uprising under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milos-obrenovic">Miloš Obrenović</a> won substantial autonomy in 1815–1816, the last Ottoman troops withdrew in 1867, and full international recognition of independence came at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> in 1878.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Serbs are predominantly Eastern Orthodox Christians, traditionally affiliated with the Serbian Orthodox Church. During the centuries of Ottoman rule it remained the only permitted Serb institution, and through its monasteries and clergy it preserved the Serbian language, literacy and national consciousness. When the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> became independent, Orthodoxy became crucial in defining national identity, since language was shared with other South Slavs.</p><p>Serbs are noted for icon painting, frescoes, literature, music and medieval architecture, shaped by Byzantine tradition. The gusle, a bowed single-stringed instrument, accompanies a centuries-old oral tradition of epic songs, most of which centre on the Ottoman occupation and the struggle for liberation. The Kosovo Myth is a central national myth that symbolises martyrdom and the defence of the homeland against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Serbs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire was a mosaic of peoples: Germans, Hungarians, Czechs, Slovaks, Poles, Ruthenians, Romanians, Croats, <b>Serbs</b>, Slovenes and Italians, with large Jewish communities in Galicia, Bohemia and Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It satisfied Hungarian aspirations while leaving the empire's other nationalities, Czechs, Slovaks, Poles, Ruthenians, Slovenes, Croats, <b>Serbs</b>, Romanians, Italians and others, without comparable autonomy, a permanent source of political tension.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Efforts in 1991 to bring the Socialist Republic of Bosnia and Herzegovina into the federation, through talks between Milošević, Bosnia's Serbian Democratic Party and the Bosniak vice-president Adil Zulfikarpašić, ended by late 1991, when Izetbegović planned a referendum on independence and violence broke out between <b>Serbs</b> and Bosniaks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After surviving the siege of Vienna in 1529, the Habsburgs built the Military Frontier, a fortified belt along the Croatian and Hungarian border settled by soldier-farmers, many of them <b>Serb</b> and Vlach refugees, and directed after 1556 by the Aulic War Council (Hofkriegsrat) in Vienna.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ustaše grew out of Croatian resentment of the centralised Kingdom of <b>Serbs</b>, Croats and Slovenes, proclaimed in 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Late medieval Hungary was a large, thinly populated kingdom of probably around three million people by 1500, including Magyars, Slovaks, Croats, Romanians, Germans, Ruthenians and <b>Serbs</b>, as well as the Saxons of Transylvania and the Szeklers and Cumans, who held distinct collective rights.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
